    {
      "raw_rank": 7,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00093",
      "distance": 1.0157119035720825,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 160,
      "page_end": 162,
      "text": "Putting rhetoric in this musical key shifts us away from rhetoric's more customary epistemological frame to an ontological one. Rhetoric is assembled, in an architectural sense, and integrated into a mode oflife-a way of being, as it were. I am going to explore this idea in three ways in the final part of the chapter, looking at fundamental mood, musical worlding, and soundmarks. Each of these terms can help illuminate our understanding of ambient rhetoric beyond the limits imposed by the discursive and visual. The point is not that we should become music composers but that bringing together music/sound, image, and discourse, while profoundly attending to the materiality of these media forms and the places they emerge within and inflect, is a crucial pursuit for rhetoric and composition today. It is less a matter of isolating various elements in order to understand their particular impact (discourse, image, meaning, mood, etc.) than of putting them together ecologically. This is one reason I avoid the term multimodal, despite its popularity. Multimodality indicates various, discrete modes that are then combined. I begin elsewhere, from the idea that such \"modes\" derive\n\nfrom an a priori ambient situation in which they are interactively combined. While we can conceptually isolate such modes, we should not then ontologize them as distinct modes that we need to put back together. In our work, wherever and however that is, we should be able to theorize, analyze, and explain how \"multimedia\" forms compose an interactive place, one no longer bounded exclusively by considerations of physical dimension or determined by overemphasis on a particular, isolated mode (such as print or sound). Such places, physical/geographic or not, are rhetorical in both their creation and their inflection. Rhetorical action is enmeshed within ambient environs, evoking (but not demanding) a rich panoply of attachments, feelings, responses, and possible (inter)actions.\n\nThis brings me back to considering what happens when a user sits down to boot Microsoft Windows. The situation will include the immediate material surroundings and ongoing activities, including the whirring, blinking computer as it boots. Then there is an affective response to the startup music, with the user conscious of that response or not. As I have related, Microsoft has expressed keen interest in this response and how it can be tailored and made meaningful. Microsoft seeks to shape the user's attunement to the Windows workspace (I use the term \"workspace\" loosely, for I don't mean to preclude forms of play, entertainment, sociality, and so forth). But we also confront a problem. If music has no essential meaning or emotional key beyond a few simple, schematic responses (\"happy,\" say, or \"sad\"), how does it evoke such specific and complex qualities as those Microsoft asked Eno and Fripp to facilitate? Recall that, as the development video shows, the team sought to evoke Vista's emotional core of\"clean, confident, connected\" by incorporating colors (green and blue) to solidify and explain the music's ephemeral impact. These colors in turn connect to specific chords or key signatures (combinations ofD and E). But this does not, in fact, answer the question, because nothing inherent in combinations of D and E will generate Microsoft's desired affective response. One could in turn point to the visuals-Vista's translucent graphics, for instance-but again, this may generate some orienting cues yet still fall short of explaining the more sculpted response Microsoft sought. Indeed. we can take this insight further by noting how limited ifnot impoverished visual and design semiotics appear from this perspective. for instance, the ubiquitous design mantra of \"contrast, repetition, alignment, proximity\" (or CRAP) originally proposed by Robin Williams. One need not abandon such semiotics, of course, for they certainly have their uses, but we would be mistaken to think that they can explain all that is necessary for successful and fulfill-\n\ning design. I will attempt a more complete explanation of these issues in the next sections, where I explore in greater detail how sonic phenomena exemplify a form of ambient rhetoric that evokes feeling, meaning, connection, community, and a sense of place-which is to say, non semiotic elements of design and rhetoric.\n\nThe Rhythm of Being: Mood",
